TLS (Transport Layer Security) is the protocol that encrypts data while it travels over a network, so nobody between
the sender and receiver can read or tamper with it. It is what makes http:// become https://.
It is the modern successor to SSL, and people often still say “SSL” when they mean TLS.

    Why it’s in your answer
    “Data in transit” means data moving between two systems, for example:
        Client to your Spring Boot API
        Your API to the database
        Your API to Azure Blob Storage

    Without TLS, anyone on the network path can read the JSON in plain text. With TLS, they see only scrambled bytes.

    Encryption at rest vs in transit
                            Protects data	Example
        At rest	            While stored	Encrypted database or storage disk
        In transit (TLS)	While moving	HTTPS call carrying the JSON

    You need both. Encrypting storage doesn’t help if the JSON was sent over plain HTTP, and TLS doesn’t help once the
     data is sitting unencrypted in storage.

    What TLS gives you
        Confidentiality: Data is encrypted, so eavesdroppers can’t read it.
        Integrity: Any tampering in transit is detected.
        Authentication: The server proves its identity with a certificate, so you know you’re talking to the real server, not an impostor.
    How it works, in short
        The client connects and the server sends its certificate.
        The client verifies the certificate against trusted authorities.
        Both sides agree on a shared secret key.
        All further data is encrypted with that key.
    In your Java/Spring context
        Expose APIs over HTTPS (server.ssl.* properties, or terminate TLS at a gateway or ingress).
        Use TLS for database connections (for example encrypt=true for SQL Server, sslmode=require for PostgreSQL).
        Use TLS 1.2 or 1.3, and disable older versions.
        mTLS (mutual TLS) goes one step further: the client also presents a certificate, which is common between internal microservices.
        Interview one-liner

“TLS encrypts data in transit between client and server, giving confidentiality, integrity and server authentication, so JSON payloads can’t be read or modified on the network.”
